        "prompt_exact": "Use the PROVIDED FIRST IMAGE (own-generated canonical deergrass) for healthy Muhlenbergia rigens morphology and subdued olive-green/straw palette. The SECOND AND THIRD images are two existing deergrass leaf-fan source parts that will be reused unchanged. MATCH their fine-bladed naturalistic pixel material, upper-left light and modest three-quarter side garden perspective. Generate ONLY ONE REPLACEMENT LOW FIBROUS BASAL ROOT CROWN, not a full grass plant or replacement upper fans. Actual RGBA with TRUE transparent background. Output1024x1024 square with the entire LOW WIDE crown centered, occupying roughly65percent width and25percent height, with generous transparent margins on every side. No text, guides, labels, UI, birds, animals, scenery, soil tile, pots, pebbles or rocks. No cast-shadow haze or background/checkerboard. Real alpha outside and through fine blade gaps.\n\nBotanically plausible NONWOODY DEERGRASS ROOT/BASE ONLY: a short dense matted fibrous bunchgrass crown with countless VERY THIN tan leaf sheaths and straw fibers, plus a collar of SHORT SLENDER GREEN BLADES that taper naturally to fine pointed tips. Root contact is a low softly uneven compressed fibrous underside; few fine root hairs at the very bottom. All straw and roots look like thin grass fibers, NOT solid cylindrical stalks. Sparse short outer blades arch low left/right, while central short fine green blades make a DENSE SOFT LEAF-SHEATH COLLAR so the narrow tan basal bundles of the supplied upper fans can later sit BEHIND this collar and be occluded naturally. Crown is broad, low and continuous, not two separate rootballs or a hollow ring. Upper fan attachment will be hidden in this natural overlapping fibrous collar, so DO NOT draw engineered sockets or visible branch ends.\n\nABSOLUTELY NO woody trunk, logs, bark, cut sticks, bamboo, thick reed tubes, posts, round cut-end disks, sawed flat stem tops, branch forks or solid stub cylinders. NO upright tall leaves that duplicate the supplied fans, no flowers, plumes, inflorescence stalks, broad iris leaves or exposed dirt block. No woodgrain or rings anywhere. This replacement fixes a previous crown incorrectly drawn as cut wood; correct it using only fine botanical grass fibers and pointed narrow green blades.\n\nDetailed restrained naturalistic PIXEL ART with deliberate crisp square clusters and stepped fine contours, controlled short value ramps, no smooth photographic airbrushing or uniform noisy dithering. Root/crown art will stay rigid while the existing separate fans rotate gently about buried base pivots. Produce one complete low fibrous root crown only, broad enough to hide both fan attachment bundles. Maintain clear visible contact underside, no clipped tips or giant decorative shadow. No newly drawn animation frames or physics claim.",
